1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,753
This guide gives you a simple race plan that stops you blowing up halfway.

2
00:00:04,753 --> 00:00:10,726
Decide your target pace before the start gun, rather than letting the pack decide it for you.

3
00:00:10,726 --> 00:00:14,130
Then hold the first third a shade easier than target.

4
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:16,764
Sit exactly on target through the middle.

5
00:00:16,764 --> 00:00:19,718
And spend whatever is left in the final third.

6
00:00:19,718 --> 00:00:33,399
Over the next few minutes you will learn how to set a target you can hold, how to turn it into one lap split, how pacing changes with distance, how to race in a pack, and how to rescue a race you started too hard.

7
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:36,165
Work it through on a real race.

8
00:00:36,165 --> 00:00:39,829
A 3000 metre race on a 200 metre track is fifteen laps.

9
00:00:39,829 --> 00:00:46,424
Go two seconds a lap quicker than target for the first three laps, and you have gained six seconds.

10
00:00:46,424 --> 00:00:52,286
Now pay for it over the remaining twelve laps, at a typical three or four seconds a lap.

11
00:00:52,286 --> 00:00:55,484
You have lost thirty six to forty eight seconds.

12
00:00:55,484 --> 00:01:02,079
When you skate above the effort you can sustain, fatigue builds faster than your body can clear it.

13
00:01:02,079 --> 00:01:05,144
Once that starts, you cannot stop it mid race.

14
00:01:05,144 --> 00:01:06,676
You can only slow down.

15
00:01:06,676 --> 00:01:08,475
There is a second cost too.

16
00:01:08,475 --> 00:01:11,472
Your technique collapses before your legs do.

17
00:01:11,472 --> 00:01:20,133
You lose depth in the knee bend, the push gets shorter, and you start skating high and choppy, right when efficiency matters most.

18
00:01:20,833 --> 00:01:23,729
This chart shows three ways a race can look.

19
00:01:23,729 --> 00:01:27,613
Each bar is your speed across five equal parts of the race.

20
00:01:27,613 --> 00:01:30,246
The orange row on top is the fast start.

21
00:01:30,246 --> 00:01:34,985
It leads early, and every part after that is slower than the one before.

22
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:36,763
It finishes slowest of all.

23
00:01:36,763 --> 00:01:39,593
The blue row in the middle is an even pace.

24
00:01:39,593 --> 00:01:43,148
The bars stay the same height all the way to the line.

25
00:01:43,148 --> 00:01:46,702
The green row at the bottom is a slightly rising pace.

26
00:01:46,702 --> 00:01:53,021
It starts a little easier and gets faster in each part, so the skater is quickest at the finish.

27
00:01:53,021 --> 00:01:56,444
The two bottom shapes are what good races look like.

28
00:01:56,444 --> 00:02:02,237
The skater who looks composed on the last lap is almost never the one who led the first.

29
00:02:02,937 --> 00:02:06,022
A target pace is useless if it comes from hope.

30
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:09,436
It has to come from something you have already done.

31
00:02:09,436 --> 00:02:16,394
The best source is a recent full effort over a similar distance, on a similar surface, in similar weather.

32
00:02:16,394 --> 00:02:19,217
Here is how to find a number you can trust.

33
00:02:19,217 --> 00:02:24,797
Skate a solo time trial over your race distance, or about half of it for a long race.

34
00:02:24,797 --> 00:02:28,079
Make it hard but controlled, and record every lap.

35
00:02:28,079 --> 00:02:33,528
Then look for the lap you could have repeated again and again, not the fastest one.

36
00:02:33,528 --> 00:02:35,891
That lap is close to your race pace.

37
00:02:35,891 --> 00:02:43,768
Rehearse that exact lap time in training with tempo repeats and short recoveries, so your legs learn what it feels like.

38
00:02:43,768 --> 00:02:47,510
And check it every few weeks, because your fitness moves.

39
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:51,040
A target time is no use to you at speed.

40
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:52,102
A lap split is.

41
00:02:52,102 --> 00:02:59,035
Divide your goal time by the number of laps, and round it to something you can read in one glance.

42
00:02:59,035 --> 00:03:03,139
Fifteen laps of a 200 metre track makes a 3000 metre race.

43
00:03:03,139 --> 00:03:08,870
A goal of five minutes is three hundred seconds, so that is twenty seconds a lap.

44
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:13,681
A 10 kilometre race on a 400 metre road circuit is twenty five laps.

45
00:03:13,681 --> 00:03:18,210
A goal of twenty minutes works out at forty eight seconds a lap.

46
00:03:18,210 --> 00:03:21,394
Write the number on your hand or your bottle.

47
00:03:21,394 --> 00:03:23,587
In a race you will not do sums.

48
00:03:23,587 --> 00:03:25,143
You will only compare.

49
00:03:25,143 --> 00:03:26,205
One more thing.

50
00:03:26,205 --> 00:03:34,978
Heat raises the cost of the same speed, so on a hot day pace the first third by trained effort and let the clock confirm it.

51
00:03:35,678 --> 00:03:38,549
Pacing is not the same for every event.

52
00:03:38,549 --> 00:03:45,175
A 300 metre time trial and a marathon ask opposite things of you in the first ten seconds.

53
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:51,726
In a 300 metre time trial, give everything from the first stride and accept a small fade.

54
00:03:51,726 --> 00:03:56,290
The common error is holding back and never reaching top speed.

55
00:03:56,290 --> 00:04:01,664
In a 500, start hard, then hold your form rather than chasing more speed.

56
00:04:01,664 --> 00:04:07,259
In a 1000, open strong but controlled, stay steady, then drive the last lap.

57
00:04:07,259 --> 00:04:13,516
For 3000 and 5000 metres, go a shade easier than target, then on target, then faster.

58
00:04:13,516 --> 00:04:19,111
In a 10000 or a points race, hold a steady base pace with surges you choose.

59
00:04:19,111 --> 00:04:25,295
In a marathon, open comfortably, settle into a group, and save your effort for late.

60
00:04:25,295 --> 00:04:29,638
Leading early because it feels easy is the classic mistake.

61
00:04:30,338 --> 00:04:36,114
Here is that plan for a distance race, split into three parts from start to finish.

62
00:04:36,114 --> 00:04:39,802
In the first third, skate a shade easier than target.

63
00:04:39,802 --> 00:04:41,472
It should feel too easy.

64
00:04:41,472 --> 00:04:42,725
That is the point.

65
00:04:42,725 --> 00:04:45,717
In the middle third, sit exactly on target.

66
00:04:45,717 --> 00:04:48,639
Check one lap time and hold your position.

67
00:04:48,639 --> 00:04:51,353
In the final third, spend what is left.

68
00:04:51,353 --> 00:04:54,276
Stop managing the race and empty the tank.

69
00:04:54,276 --> 00:04:56,851
Look at the warning along the bottom.

70
00:04:56,851 --> 00:05:00,956
If the first third already feels hard, the plan has failed.

71
00:05:00,956 --> 00:05:03,531
Start easing off now, not at halfway.

72
00:05:03,531 --> 00:05:08,680
The same three part plan works from 1000 metres all the way to a marathon.

73
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:12,507
Only the size of the margin between the phases changes.

74
00:05:13,207 --> 00:05:18,139
A bad corner can cost you as much as a mispaced first lap, only more quietly.

75
00:05:18,139 --> 00:05:25,313
If your splits swing by a second or more while your effort stays even, look at your corners before your fitness.

76
00:05:25,313 --> 00:05:28,195
Carry speed into the bend and hold your line.

77
00:05:28,195 --> 00:05:33,447
Otherwise an even effort turns into a series of small sprints down every straight.

78
00:05:33,447 --> 00:05:35,689
Pack racing is a different problem.

79
00:05:35,689 --> 00:05:41,454
You no longer control your own speed, so your job becomes choosing which surges to follow.

80
00:05:41,454 --> 00:05:45,553
Sitting in a group costs noticeably less effort than leading it.

81
00:05:45,553 --> 00:05:48,627
A surge that opens a real gap has to be covered.

82
00:05:48,627 --> 00:05:52,919
A skater who attacks every few laps and always comes back does not.

83
00:05:52,919 --> 00:05:59,772
And if you are only just hanging on at the back while the pace is steady, drop out and skate your own pace.

84
00:05:59,772 --> 00:06:02,398
You will almost always get a better time.

85
00:06:03,098 --> 00:06:04,742
It happens to everybody.

86
00:06:04,742 --> 00:06:09,468
The race is not over, but the next lap or two decide how bad it gets.

87
00:06:09,468 --> 00:06:13,714
First, back off gradually instead of stopping the effort dead.

88
00:06:13,714 --> 00:06:17,139
Next, switch from your lap time to your technique.

89
00:06:17,139 --> 00:06:22,207
Get the knee bend back, lengthen the push, and let the recovery leg relax.

90
00:06:22,207 --> 00:06:24,604
Form is what you can still control.

91
00:06:24,604 --> 00:06:27,001
Then let go of the original target.

92
00:06:27,001 --> 00:06:33,303
Chasing a time that is already gone turns a slightly disappointing race into a very bad one.

93
00:06:33,303 --> 00:06:37,481
Find a group going a little slower than you were, and sit in.

94
00:06:37,481 --> 00:06:41,727
Recovering behind someone is far easier than recovering alone.

95
00:06:41,727 --> 00:06:44,672
And afterwards, write your lap splits down.

96
00:06:44,672 --> 00:06:49,055
A badly paced race is excellent data if you actually look at it.

97
00:06:49,755 --> 00:06:53,404
Pacing is a skill, not a purchase, with one exception.

98
00:06:53,404 --> 00:06:57,660
Race on the frame and wheel platform you did your pace work on.

99
00:06:57,660 --> 00:07:03,403
Wheel size changes how far each stride carries you, and so your stride count per lap.

100
00:07:03,403 --> 00:07:08,673
That is the rhythm your legs have memorised, so do not change it in race week.

101
00:07:08,673 --> 00:07:09,686
Now, your body.

102
00:07:09,686 --> 00:07:14,889
Heavy breathing, burning legs and a thumping heart are normal in a hard race.

103
00:07:14,889 --> 00:07:21,239
Chest pain or pressure, dizziness, feeling faint, or an unusually irregular heartbeat are not.

104
00:07:21,239 --> 00:07:24,888
Neither are symptoms that do not settle once you stop.

105
00:07:24,888 --> 00:07:29,144
Those are reasons to stop the race and get checked by a doctor.

106
00:07:29,144 --> 00:07:34,819
The same goes for pain that lasts more than a few days, or any swelling or numbness.

107
00:07:35,519 --> 00:07:38,092
If you remember one thing, make it this.

108
00:07:38,092 --> 00:07:44,653
Decide your pace before the gun, keep the first third a shade easy, and spend what is left at the end.

109
00:07:44,653 --> 00:07:49,477
In a pack, choose which surges you follow instead of answering all of them.

110
00:07:49,477 --> 00:07:57,453
And if you go out too fast, back off gradually and rebuild your technique, rather than chasing a time that has already gone.

111
00:07:57,453 --> 00:08:02,985
Pace a race well three or four times, and you will stop needing the plan written down.

112
00:08:02,985 --> 00:08:08,002
The full guide, with answers on heats and new tracks, is on the LiveLica blog.

113
00:08:08,002 --> 00:08:09,995
The link is in the description.
